{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQphyaBrPbg4bfPMy6Q1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your answer settles two things. Customers pick 2, 3, or 4 installments, and the installments are equal. Equal installments mean a fixed schedule, so each installment gets its own due date (Q1 option a). That releases the held questions about the schedule and overdue status.\n\n**Settled**\n- Fixed schedule: 2, 3, or 4 equal installments, each with its own due date.\n- The customer chooses the count.\n- Invoices without a plan behave exactly as today.\n\n**Mine** (unchanged): leftover cents go on the last installment; payments cover the oldest installment first; the invoice becomes `paid` at the full total; each installment gets a reminder 3 days before it's due.\n\n**Questions**\n\n1. **Can customers start a plan on any invoice?** You said they choose the count. What's still open is whether they can turn a plan on themselves.\n   - **(a) Yes**, on any eligible invoice.\n   - **(b) Only where staff have turned plans on** for that invoice or customer.\n   - *Recommendation: (b).* A plan gives the customer extra time to pay, so you keep control over who gets that.\n\n2. **Which invoices are eligible?**\n   - **(a) Only `sent` invoices before their due date.**\n   - **(b) Overdue invoices too.** You'd then also need to decide what happens to the 2% fee already added.\n   - *Recommendation: (a).* It keeps plans and late fees separate.\n\n3. **When are the installments due?**\n   - **(a) The first is due on the original due date, then one every month after.** A 4-part plan ends 3 months later than the invoice would have. This gives the customer real extra time, which is the usual point of installments.\n   - **(b) Spread out between when the plan starts and the original due date.** The last one falls on the original due date, so you wait no longer to be paid in full. But the gaps can be very short, and close to the due date there's no room for any.\n   - **(c) The first is due when the plan starts, then one every month.**\n   - *Recommendation: (a).* It is predictable and simple to show the customer.\n\n4. **When does an invoice on a plan become `overdue`?**\n   - **(a) As soon as any installment is past due and unpaid.** You find out about trouble early, and the meaning of the status doesn't change.\n   - **(b) Only when the final installment is missed.** This is lenient, but a missed first payment shows nothing for months.\n   - *Recommendation: (a).*\n\n**Held**\n- Late fee under a plan: 2% of the missed installment, or 2% of the full total once as today. This waits on Q4, because when the invoice becomes overdue changes what the fee should be charged on."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2261,"cache_read_input_tokens":38503,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2261},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5a99d714-fdae-4c3f-923a-d7e81801feb1","uuid":"19122c82-2cf7-4216-a3f4-c9ca5e992d69","timestamp":"2026-11-26T03:23:53.635Z","request_id":"req_011ChQphyRkvHiZYKzPqWzBd"}
